    "body": "Two defects that together made every exception message invisible in the logs, found while running the PC/SC examples of `keyple-cpp-example`.\n\nBoth are divergences from the Java reference implementation, not Windows workarounds.\n\n### Commits\n\n- `d9db257` **report the detail message through `what()`.** `Exception` inherits `std::exception` but only exposed `getMessage()`, so every generic `catch` reported the default \"Unknown exception\" and the real cause was invisible. In Java the message is reachable from the base class, which is what makes a generic handler useful.\n- `18638c3` **log C strings in full.** A `const char*` argument was captured by the generic pointer overload of `Logger::printf`, which dereferences its argument, so only the first character of the string was logged. A message obtained from `what()` came out as a single letter.\n\n### Why they belong together\n\nThe first fix makes `what()` return the message; without the second, the very logger used to report it printed one character. Each one alone leaves the diagnostic unusable. With both in place, a caller catching `std::exception` finally sees the cause, which is how the other porting defects in the C++ libraries became diagnosable at all.\n\n### Validation\n\nValidated on hardware across the whole example suite: the messages that used to read \"Unknown exception\" or a lone letter now carry the full cause, including the card and SAM status words.\n",
